Article 18

1. The Regional Government in efforts to protect women is responsible for:a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women victims of violence;
d. increasing efforts to protect female workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing women's protection services;
f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or community non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent acts of violence against women; andg. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities and community social institutions in the field of women's protection.
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus implementing Government Affairs in the field of women's and children's empowerment and in its implementation may coordinate with relevant agencies.

Article 19

In order to prevent acts of violence against women, the Regional Government is obliged to:
a. conduct socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provide guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
c. involve community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. develop an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women;
e. strengthen the function of community organizations engaged in the empowerment of women and children; andf. provide public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women.

Article 5

In addition to the rights of women and children as referred to in Article 3 and Article 4, women and children victims of violence have the right to:
a. have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to complaints and fast and affordable services;
c. obtain information and legal certainty;
d. confidentiality of the case being handled;
e. compensation for the case being handled;
f. obtain facilities in the judicial process;
g. obtain assistance;
h. obtain physical and psychological health recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
i. determine their own decisions;
j. obtain rehabilitation and social reintegration;
k. obtain respect and services for survival, growth and development;
l. obtain basic services;
m. be free from negative stigma; andn. obtain freedom to determine choices to decide for themselves after their parents' divorce;

Article 20

1. Protection of women victims of violence is carried out through integrated services.
2. Provision of integrated services for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; andl. fulfillment of women's rights.

Article 25

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women victims of violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.
3. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by:a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the social sector;
b. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the sector of women's and children's empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or existing social community organizations; orc. religious institutions and/or organizations.

Article 22

Integrated complaint handling services as referred to in Article 21 letter a are carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. women's service units;
d. trauma center shelters;
e. women crisis centers;
f. marriage counseling, guidance and preservation boards;
g. police;
h. prosecutor's office;
i. courts;
j. ministry of religion; and/ork. legal aid institutions.
1. Complaints of acts of violence against women may be made by reporters consisting of victims, families, communities and/or community organizations in the field of women's and children's protection.
2. Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may file complaints:a. directly;
b. by telephone; and/orc. by letter.
3. In addition to handling complaints submitted by reporters, officers at service institutions as referred to in Article 22 are also authorized to handle victims of acts of violence against women obtained through other information.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for complaint handling services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 4

The rights of children as referred to in Article 2 include:
a. obtaining education and teaching according to age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence and interests and talents;
b. living, growing, developing, and participating reasonably in accordance with human dignity and receiving protection from acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination and neglect, cruelty, violence and abuse, injustice and mistreatment;
c. having needs fulfilled by the family;
d. obtaining religious guidance;
e. obtaining their identity;
f. obtaining other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
g. specifically for children with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in their services;

Article 24

1. Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:a. promotive;
b. preventive;
c. curative; andd. rehabilitative.
2. Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, can be accessed 24 (twenty-four) hours at the Regional General Hospital, is of quality and is carried out in accordance with health service standards.
3. Women victims of violence have the right to free health services, including:a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services; andc. visum.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 27

1. The Regional Government may provide safe houses for women victims of violence.
2. The provision of safe house services as referred to in Article 25 paragraph (2) letter b aims to protect women victims of violence from intimidation by perpetrators or other parties.
3. The location of the safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) must be kept confidential.
4. The criteria for women victims of violence who require safe houses include:a. their life is threatened;
b. receiving rejection from family or community;
c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far away; and/ord. abandoned if not placed in a safe house.
5. The provision of safe house services is carried out by social rehabilitation officers who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness, consisting of:a. social workers/volunteers;
b. security officers; and/orc. community leaders.
6. Further provisions regarding the provision of safe house services are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

I. GENERAL

The 1945 Constitution as a constitutional foundation has explicitly regulated the importance of protection of Human Rights, including the rights of women and children. In addition to the guarantee from the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the guarantee that every person must have equal dignity and degree has also been recognized internationally. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights or UDHR of 1948 which underlies Human Rights in general has guaranteed that all people are born with freedom, having equal dignity and rights without exception.

The 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia mandates the Regional Government to regulate and administer regional government affairs aimed at realizing community welfare through improved services, protection and empowerment of the community. As a component of society that plays an important role in realizing a quality generation and as successors to the nation's struggle, women and children must receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights.

To ensure the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection in accordance with human dignity and in order to carry out mandatory affairs, the regional government needs to provide legal certainty in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection through the formulation of a policy. The formulation of such policy is a form of support and concrete effort from various parties, starting from the Regional Government, community and other parties in ensuring the fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is carried out based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination: b. the best interests of women and children;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the opinion of the child; e. Legal certainty;
f. local wisdom; and justice and gender equality.

The formulation of the Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to:
a. provide guidance in the planning and implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection policies;
b. increase efforts for Women's Empowerment and fulfillment of children's rights in order to realize improved quality of life for women and children in the Region;
c. increase efforts to protect women and children from actions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;
d. increase the value of local wisdom and the role of custom in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and e. increase the role of the Regional Government and other community institutions in efforts for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

This Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children regulates:
a. Improving the quality of life of women which contains government responsibility, implementation of gender mainstreaming, efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the political, legal, social and economic fields;
b. Protection of women which contains the responsibility of regional government in the protection of women, prevention of violence against women, protection for women victims of violence, and complaint handling;
c. Protection of children which contains the responsibility of regional government, parents/family and community in the protection of children and efforts made by the regional government in implementing child protection. In addition, the need to establish a children's forum and Child-Friendly Regency as a form of appreciation in realizing protection and fulfillment of children's rights.

Article 30

1. Law enforcement and legal assistance services are intended to provide assistance and protection to women victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.
2. Types of legal assistance services include:a. legal assistance in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;
b. legal assistance in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal assistance in the examination process at court;
d. legal assistance in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; ande. legal assistance in the mediation process.
3. The Regional Government provides legal assistance to women victims of violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. In providing legal assistance as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other gender-sensitive legal assistants.

Article 26

1. Counseling services as referred to in Article 25 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:a. assist women victims of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower women victims of violence to determine their future;
c. provide moral support to women victims of violence in facing the process they are undergoing; andd. make women victims of violence feel accepted in the community environment.
2. Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:a. counselors;
b. psychologists; andc. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women victims of violence.
4. The stages of counseling services include:a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; andd. site visits.

Article 58

1. Regional Governments are obliged to provide social welfare for:a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of human trafficking;
c. children who do not have parents and are cared for by parties or families who are unable;
d. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; ande. children with disabilities;
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of providing services:a. health;
b. education;
c. legal aid;
d. assistance;
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation; and/org. reintegration.

Article 71

1. Community participation in efforts to protect women and children:a. participates in prevention and provides support for the development of programs to prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
b. provides protection to victims and emergency assistance;
c. provides information from/or reports violations of the rights of women and children to the competent authorities; andd. monitors the law enforcement process for cases of violations of the rights of women and children.
2. Family and Parents are responsible for protecting and preventing violations of the rights of women and children.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be based on laws and regulations and the values of local wisdom of the local community.

CHAPTER VIII

GENDER AND CHILD DATA SYSTEM

Article 69

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection to children:
a. prevents violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
b. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling on child protection and rights;
c. develops child-friendly school models;
d. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children;
e. compiles databases and information on acts of violence against children; f. maps locations and areas prone to violence against children;
g. coordinates and cooperates in preventing violations of children's rights;
h. strengthens the function of institutions or organizations working in the field of child protection; andi. encourages public awareness of the importance of child protection by establishing women and child protection assistants.

Service efforts undertaken by the Regional Government for women victims of violence as referred to in Article 20 through Article 33 shall apply mutatis mutandis to service efforts for women victims of violence.

Article 3

The rights of women as referred to in Article 2 include:
a. living peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously physically and spiritually;
b. enjoying a good and healthy living environment;
c. living in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. obtaining work according to ability, appropriate conditions and fair and just wages;
e. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services;
f. playing an active role in the field of politics and government according to their potential;
g. obtaining information and legal services;
h. obtaining services to increase their income;
i. obtaining other rights according to their humanitarian benefits based on the provisions of laws and regulations;

