a. communication, information and education;
b. contraceptive devices and medicines;
c. recording and reporting of family planning services;
d. adequate health service facilities and infrastructure; ande. breastfeeding corners in every public facility.

Article 54

1. Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 53 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving the degree of health more optimally.
2. Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food;
e. the dangers of drugs and smoking; and (3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Governments may carry out other activities in the context of health provision.

Article 55

1. Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 53 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting periodic health examinations; andf. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school.

Article 21

Forms of integrated services for women victims of violence include:
a. complaint handling;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance; ande. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 25

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women victims of violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.
3. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by:a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the social sector;
b. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the sector of women's and children's empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or existing social community organizations; orc. religious institutions and/or organizations.

