CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

I. GENERAL

The 1945 Constitution as a constitutional foundation has explicitly regulated the importance of protection of Human Rights, including the rights of women and children. In addition to the guarantee from the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the guarantee that every person must have equal dignity and degree has also been recognized internationally. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights or UDHR of 1948 which underlies Human Rights in general has guaranteed that all people are born with freedom, having equal dignity and rights without exception.

The 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia mandates the Regional Government to regulate and administer regional government affairs aimed at realizing community welfare through improved services, protection and empowerment of the community. As a component of society that plays an important role in realizing a quality generation and as successors to the nation's struggle, women and children must receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights.

To ensure the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection in accordance with human dignity and in order to carry out mandatory affairs, the regional government needs to provide legal certainty in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection through the formulation of a policy. The formulation of such policy is a form of support and concrete effort from various parties, starting from the Regional Government, community and other parties in ensuring the fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is carried out based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination: b. the best interests of women and children;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the opinion of the child; e. Legal certainty;
f. local wisdom; and justice and gender equality.

The formulation of the Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to:
a. provide guidance in the planning and implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection policies;
b. increase efforts for Women's Empowerment and fulfillment of children's rights in order to realize improved quality of life for women and children in the Region;
c. increase efforts to protect women and children from actions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;
d. increase the value of local wisdom and the role of custom in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and e. increase the role of the Regional Government and other community institutions in efforts for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

This Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children regulates:
a. Improving the quality of life of women which contains government responsibility, implementation of gender mainstreaming, efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the political, legal, social and economic fields;
b. Protection of women which contains the responsibility of regional government in the protection of women, prevention of violence against women, protection for women victims of violence, and complaint handling;
c. Protection of children which contains the responsibility of regional government, parents/family and community in the protection of children and efforts made by the regional government in implementing child protection. In addition, the need to establish a children's forum and Child-Friendly Regency as a form of appreciation in realizing protection and fulfillment of children's rights.

Article 7

1. In order to improve the quality of life of women, the regional government is responsible for:a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. implementing gender mainstreaming;
c. establishing a gender mainstreaming working group;
d. increasing the empowerment of women in roles, quality and capacity in the fields of:
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.  e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for women's empowerment in the Region;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 71

1. Community participation in efforts to protect women and children:a. participates in prevention and provides support for the development of programs to prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
b. provides protection to victims and emergency assistance;
c. provides information from/or reports violations of the rights of women and children to the competent authorities; andd. monitors the law enforcement process for cases of violations of the rights of women and children.
2. Family and Parents are responsible for protecting and preventing violations of the rights of women and children.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be based on laws and regulations and the values of local wisdom of the local community.

Article 5

In addition to the rights of women and children as referred to in Article 3 and Article 4, women and children victims of violence have the right to:
a. have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to complaints and fast and affordable services;
c. obtain information and legal certainty;
d. confidentiality of the case being handled;
e. compensation for the case being handled;
f. obtain facilities in the judicial process;
g. obtain assistance;
h. obtain physical and psychological health recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
i. determine their own decisions;
j. obtain rehabilitation and social reintegration;
k. obtain respect and services for survival, growth and development;
l. obtain basic services;
m. be free from negative stigma; andn. obtain freedom to determine choices to decide for themselves after their parents' divorce;

Article 25

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women victims of violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.
3. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by:a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the social sector;
b. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the sector of women's and children's empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or existing social community organizations; orc. religious institutions and/or organizations.

Article 19

In order to prevent acts of violence against women, the Regional Government is obliged to:
a. conduct socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provide guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
c. involve community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. develop an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women;
e. strengthen the function of community organizations engaged in the empowerment of women and children; andf. provide public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women.

Article 6

The implementation of women's empowerment and child protection is based on the principles of:
a. the best interests of women and children;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom;
d. law enforcement and legal certainty;
e. non-discrimination;
f. the right to life, survival, and development;
g. respect for the views of children; and

Article 24

1. Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:a. promotive;
b. preventive;
c. curative; andd. rehabilitative.
2. Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, can be accessed 24 (twenty-four) hours at the Regional General Hospital, is of quality and is carried out in accordance with health service standards.
3. Women victims of violence have the right to free health services, including:a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services; andc. visum.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

