CHAPTER VIII

GENDER AND CHILD DATA SYSTEM

Article 69

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection to children:
a. prevents violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
b. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling on child protection and rights;
c. develops child-friendly school models;
d. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children;
e. compiles databases and information on acts of violence against children; f. maps locations and areas prone to violence against children;
g. coordinates and cooperates in preventing violations of children's rights;
h. strengthens the function of institutions or organizations working in the field of child protection; andi. encourages public awareness of the importance of child protection by establishing women and child protection assistants.

Service efforts undertaken by the Regional Government for women victims of violence as referred to in Article 20 through Article 33 shall apply mutatis mutandis to service efforts for women victims of violence.

Article 30

1. Law enforcement and legal assistance services are intended to provide assistance and protection to women victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.
2. Types of legal assistance services include:a. legal assistance in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;
b. legal assistance in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal assistance in the examination process at court;
d. legal assistance in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; ande. legal assistance in the mediation process.
3. The Regional Government provides legal assistance to women victims of violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. In providing legal assistance as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other gender-sensitive legal assistants.

Article 3

The rights of women as referred to in Article 2 include:
a. living peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously physically and spiritually;
b. enjoying a good and healthy living environment;
c. living in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. obtaining work according to ability, appropriate conditions and fair and just wages;
e. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services;
f. playing an active role in the field of politics and government according to their potential;
g. obtaining information and legal services;
h. obtaining services to increase their income;
i. obtaining other rights according to their humanitarian benefits based on the provisions of laws and regulations;

Article 71

1. Community participation in efforts to protect women and children:a. participates in prevention and provides support for the development of programs to prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
b. provides protection to victims and emergency assistance;
c. provides information from/or reports violations of the rights of women and children to the competent authorities; andd. monitors the law enforcement process for cases of violations of the rights of women and children.
2. Family and Parents are responsible for protecting and preventing violations of the rights of women and children.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be based on laws and regulations and the values of local wisdom of the local community.

Article 25

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women victims of violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.
3. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by:a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the social sector;
b. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the sector of women's and children's empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or existing social community organizations; orc. religious institutions and/or organizations.

Article 19

In order to prevent acts of violence against women, the Regional Government is obliged to:
a. conduct socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provide guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
c. involve community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. develop an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women;
e. strengthen the function of community organizations engaged in the empowerment of women and children; andf. provide public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women.

Article 21

Forms of integrated services for women victims of violence include:
a. complaint handling;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance; ande. repatriation and social reintegration.

