Article 58

1. Regional Governments are obliged to provide social welfare for:a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of human trafficking;
c. children who do not have parents and are cared for by parties or families who are unable;
d. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; ande. children with disabilities;
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of providing services:a. health;
b. education;
c. legal aid;
d. assistance;
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation; and/org. reintegration.

Article 13

1. The gender mainstreaming focal point in each Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 11 letter 1 consists of officials and/or staff in charge of planning and/or program tasks.
2. The gender mainstreaming focal point as referred to in paragraph (1), has the tasks of:a. promoting gender mainstreaming in the work unit;
b. facilitating the preparation of work plans and budgeting of Regional Apparatus that are gender-responsive;
c. conducting training, socialization, advocacy of gender mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the Regional Apparatus;
d. reporting the implementation of gender mainstreaming to the leadership of the Regional Apparatus; e. encouraging the implementation of gender analysis of policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; andf. facilitating the preparation of gender data in each Regional Apparatus.
3. The gender mainstreaming focal point as referred to in paragraph (1), is selected and appointed by the Regional Apparatus.

Article 18

1. The Regional Government in efforts to protect women is responsible for:a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women victims of violence;
d. increasing efforts to protect female workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing women's protection services;
f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or community non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent acts of violence against women; andg. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities and community social institutions in the field of women's protection.
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus implementing Government Affairs in the field of women's and children's empowerment and in its implementation may coordinate with relevant agencies.

Article 54

1. Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 53 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving the degree of health more optimally.
2. Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food;
e. the dangers of drugs and smoking; and (3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Governments may carry out other activities in the context of health provision.

Article 7

1. In order to improve the quality of life of women, the regional government is responsible for:a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. implementing gender mainstreaming;
c. establishing a gender mainstreaming working group;
d. increasing the empowerment of women in roles, quality and capacity in the fields of:
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.  e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for women's empowerment in the Region;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 25

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 21 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women victims of violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.
3. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by:a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the social sector;
b. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the sector of women's and children's empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or existing social community organizations; orc. religious institutions and/or organizations.

Article 30

1. Law enforcement and legal assistance services are intended to provide assistance and protection to women victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.
2. Types of legal assistance services include:a. legal assistance in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;
b. legal assistance in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal assistance in the examination process at court;
d. legal assistance in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; ande. legal assistance in the mediation process.
3. The Regional Government provides legal assistance to women victims of violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. In providing legal assistance as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other gender-sensitive legal assistants.

Article 12

1. The Technical Team as referred to in Article 11 letter j consists of officials who understand gender-responsive budget analysis.
2. The regional action plan for regional gender mainstreaming as referred to in Article 11 letter k contains:a. gender mainstreaming in laws and regulations;
b. gender mainstreaming in the development cycle;
c. strengthening gender mainstreaming institutions; andd. strengthening community participation.

Article 47

1. Regional Government in efforts to fulfill children's rights is responsible for:a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for fulfillment of children's rights;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the child, birth order of the child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing children's rights;
d. guaranteeing protection, maintenance, and welfare of children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons legally responsible for the child;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the administration of fulfillment of children's rights;
f. guaranteeing the right of children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the child;
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of administering fulfillment of children's rights;
h. conducting cooperation with private parties and/or NGOs in the administration of child protection;
2. Government responsibility as referred to in the administration of Institutional and Government Protection in the field of Child Empowerment.
